<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<pretext>
  <docinfo>
    <latex-image-preamble>
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\tikzset{
    flow vertex/.style={
        circle, draw=black, fill=white, semithick,
        minimum size=6.5mm, inner sep=0pt, font=\small\normalfont
    },
    flow edge/.style={
        -{Stealth[length=2mm,width=1.4mm]}, semithick, draw=black
    },
    flow weight/.style={
        fill=white, inner sep=1.3pt, font=\small\normalfont
    },
    inactive vertex/.style={
        flow vertex, draw=gray!65, text=gray!65
    },
    inactive edge/.style={flow edge, draw=gray, opacity=0.35},
    inactive weight/.style={flow weight, text=gray, text opacity=0.65}
}
    </latex-image-preamble>
  </docinfo>

  <article>
    <title>Reaching a Flow State</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-review">
      <title>Review</title>

      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>T_{1}: U \to V</m> and <m>T_{2}: U \to V</m> be linear transformations.
            Define <m>T: U \to V^{2}</m> by
            <m>T(\vec{u}) = (T_{1}(\vec{u}), T_{2}(\vec{u}))</m>.
            Then <m>T</m> is a linear transformation.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-graphs">
      <title>Graphs</title>

      <p>
        A <term>directed graph</term> is a finite collection of nodes <m>N</m> and arrows
        between nodes <m>E \subseteq N \times N</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-directed-graphs">
        <caption/>
        <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
          <figure>
            <caption>A directed path.</caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>Three vertices a, b, and c arranged in a row, with directed edges from a to b and from b to c.</shortdescription>
              <latex-image label="directed-path">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-0.45) rectangle (3.4,2.2);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0.9) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (1.5,0.9) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (3,0.9) {$c$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- (c);
\end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <caption>A directed cycle.</caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>Three vertices a, b, and c forming a triangle, with directed edges a to b, b to c, and c to a.</shortdescription>
              <latex-image label="directed-cycle">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-0.45) rectangle (3.4,2.2);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (3,0) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (1.5,1.8) {$c$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </sidebyside>
      </figure>

      <p>
        We are interested in <term>weighted</term> directed graphs, which additionally have a
        function <m>w: E \to \mathbb{R}</m> giving a weight to each edge.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-weighted-directed-graphs">
        <caption/>
        <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
          <figure>
            <caption>Positive edge weights.</caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>A directed triangle with edge weights 2, 3, and 1.</shortdescription>
              <latex-image label="weighted-positive">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-0.45) rectangle (3.4,2.2);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (3,0) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (1.5,1.8) {$c$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below] {$2$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$3$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$1$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <caption>General real edge weights.</caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>A directed triangle with edge weights negative 1, three halves, and 0.</shortdescription>
              <latex-image label="weighted-real">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-0.45) rectangle (3.4,2.2);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (3,0) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (1.5,1.8) {$c$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below] {$-1$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$\tfrac{3}{2}$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$0$} (b);
\end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </sidebyside>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Today we are going to model having water or electricity or Amazon packages flow through a
        graph. There is a key property such a flow has. At any node of the network the total weight
        of what comes in must equal the total weight of what comes out.
      </p>

      <p>
        The in-flow at a node <m>n</m> of a graph is the sum of weights of all edges going into
        <m>n</m>, and we denote it <m>\text{in-flow}(n)</m>. The
        <m>\text{out-flow}(n)</m> is the sum of weights of edges going out. A weighting of a directed
        graph is called a <term>circulation</term> if in-flow is equal to out-flow at all vertices
        of the graph.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-circulation-examples">
        <caption/>
        <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
          <figure>
            <caption>A circulation. At <m>a</m>, <m>5=2+3</m>; flow is conserved at every vertex.</caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>A four-vertex directed graph with weights 2, 2, 3, 3, and 5 that satisfies flow conservation at every vertex.</shortdescription>
              <latex-image label="circulation-example">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-1.75) rectangle (3.8,1.75);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (1.7,1.35) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (3.4,0) {$c$};
    \node[flow vertex] (d) at (1.7,-1.35) {$d$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$2$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$2$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below left] {$3$} (d);
    \draw[flow edge] (d) -- node[flow weight,below right] {$3$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- node[flow weight,above] {$5$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <caption>Not a circulation. At <m>a</m>, the in-flow is <m>4</m>, but the out-flow is <m>5</m>.</caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>A four-vertex directed graph with weights 2, 2, 3, 3, and 4 that fails flow conservation at vertex a.</shortdescription>
              <latex-image label="circulation-nonexample">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-1.75) rectangle (3.8,1.75);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (1.7,1.35) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (3.4,0) {$c$};
    \node[flow vertex] (d) at (1.7,-1.35) {$d$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$2$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$2$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below left] {$3$} (d);
    \draw[flow edge] (d) -- node[flow weight,below right] {$3$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- node[flow weight,above] {$4$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </sidebyside>
      </figure>

      <p>
        For a graph <m>G = (N,E)</m>, we let <m>\mathbb{R}^{E}</m> denote the set of weightings of
        the edges of <m>G</m>.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-circulations-subspace">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>G = (N,E)</m> be a directed graph. The set of
            <m>\vec{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{E}</m> for which <m>\vec{w}</m> is a circulation is a linear
            subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^{E}</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            For each node <m>v \in N</m>, define a linear transformation
            <m>T_v:\mathbb{R}^{E}\to\mathbb{R}</m> by
            <m>T_v(\vec{w})=\text{in-flow}(v)-\text{out-flow}(v)</m>.
            Define <m>T:\mathbb{R}^{E}\to\mathbb{R}^{N}</m> by
            <m>T(\vec{w})=(T_v(\vec{w}))_{v\in N}</m>.
            By definition, a weighting of the edges <m>\vec{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{E}</m> is a
            circulation if and only if <m>T_v(\vec{w}) = 0</m> for all <m>v \in N</m>.
            Thus, the circulations are exactly <m>\ker(T)</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        Unlike my freestyle rapping, when we talk about graphs, one cannot have negative flow.
        Thus, we will only concern ourselves with edge weights that are all non-negative. Thus, the
        set of non-negative circulations is
        <m>\ker(T) \cap \mathbb{R}^{E}_{\geq 0}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <term>indicator vector</term> of a subset <m>S</m> of edges of a graph is
        <m>\chi_{S}</m>, defined by <m>\chi_{S}(e) = 1</m> if <m>e \in S</m> and <m>0</m>
        otherwise. A <term>cycle</term> in a graph is a path that starts and ends at the same point
        and does not repeat any vertex other than its start.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cycle-indicator">
        <caption>
          The black cycle is <m>C=(a,b,c,d,a)</m>. Its indicator weighting is <m>1</m> on the cycle
          and <m>0</m> elsewhere.
        </caption>
        <image width="70%">
          <shortdescription>A larger directed graph containing the cycle a to b to c to d to a. Cycle edges are black with weight 1; all other edges are gray and translucent with weight 0.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image label="cycle-indicator">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (2,1.55) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (4,0) {$c$};
    \node[flow vertex] (d) at (2,-1.55) {$d$};
    \node[inactive vertex] (e) at (-1.5,1.55) {$e$};
    \node[inactive vertex] (f) at (5.5,1.55) {$f$};
    \draw[inactive edge] (e) -- node[inactive weight,above left] {$0$} (a);
    \draw[inactive edge] (e) -- node[inactive weight,above] {$0$} (b);
    \draw[inactive edge] (b) -- node[inactive weight,above] {$0$} (f);
    \draw[inactive edge] (f) -- node[inactive weight,above right] {$0$} (c);
    \draw[inactive edge] (a) -- node[inactive weight,above] {$0$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$1$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$1$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- node[flow weight,below right] {$1$} (d);
    \draw[flow edge] (d) -- node[flow weight,below left] {$1$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-cyclecirculation">
        <statement>
          <p>The indicator vector of any cycle is a circulation.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Consider a vertex of a cycle. Then it has one incoming edge of the cycle and one outgoing
            edge of the cycle. Thus, the net flow is <m>0</m> at each vertex, so it is a circulation.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </lemma>

      <p>
        We call the <term>support</term> of a weighting <m>\vec{w}</m>
        <m>\operatorname{supp}(\vec{w}) = \{e \in E: \vec{w}_{e} \neq 0\}</m>, the set of edges
        with nonzero weight. It turns out that the support of any non-negative circulation must
        contain a cycle.
      </p>

      <lemma xml:id="lem-cyclecontainment">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>G = (N,E)</m> be a directed graph. Let
            <m>\vec{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{E}</m> be a non-negative circulation. Then if
            <m>\operatorname{supp}(\vec{w}) \neq \emptyset</m>, it contains a cycle.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Let <m>S \subseteq N</m> be the set of vertices of <m>N</m> with an incoming edge of
            positive weight. Since <m>\operatorname{supp}(\vec{w}) \neq \emptyset</m>, there exists
            at least one vertex with an incoming edge of positive weight, meaning that
            <m>S \neq \emptyset</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Let <m>v \in S</m>. Then the in-flow of <m>v</m> is positive, so by flow conservation,
            the out-flow must also be positive. Therefore, it must have an outgoing edge of positive
            weight. Furthermore, the vertex reached by moving along that edge must also be in
            <m>S</m>, as it has an incoming edge of positive weight.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then one can generate a path in the graph starting at a vertex in <m>S</m> and always
            choosing an outgoing edge of positive weight. Since the graph is finite, this path will
            eventually reach a vertex that has already been seen and thus contain a cycle.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </lemma>

      <p>
        In fact, a stronger property holds: the indicator vectors of cycles play an analogous role
        to a basis for non-negative circulations.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-cycle-decomposition">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>G = (N,E)</m> be a directed graph. Let
            <m>\vec{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{E}</m>. Then <m>\vec{w}</m> is a non-negative circulation if
            and only if it is a non-negative linear combination of indicator vectors of cycles.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            By <xref ref="lem-cyclecirculation"/>, any indicator vector of a cycle is a circulation.
            Furthermore, the set of circulations is a linear subspace, so any linear combination is
            a circulation. Any non-negative linear combination of non-negative circulations will
            similarly be a non-negative circulation. Therefore, any non-negative linear combination
            of indicator vectors of cycles is a non-negative circulation.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the other direction, let <m>\vec{w} \in \mathbb{R}^{E}</m> be a non-negative
            circulation. Then by <xref ref="lem-cyclecontainment"/>, its support contains a cycle
            <m>C</m>. Take <m>\vec{w} - t\chi_{C}</m>, where <m>t</m> is the minimum weight of any
            edge on that cycle. Then <m>\vec{w} - t\chi_{C}</m> is still a non-negative circulation
            but has at least one fewer nonzero-weight edge. Repeating this at most <m>|E|</m> times
            yields
          </p>
          <me>\vec{w} - \sum_{i=1}^{k} t_{i} \chi_{C_{i}} = \vec{0}</me>
          <p>
            for some <m>t_{i} &gt; 0</m> and cycles <m>C_{1},\dots,C_{k}</m>. Thus,
          </p>
          <me>\vec{w} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} t_{i} \chi_{C_{i}}</me>
          <p>
            and is therefore a non-negative linear combination of indicator vectors of cycles.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-exercises">
      <title>Exercises</title>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-circulations">
        <statement>
          <p>Consider the following weighted directed graphs. Which weightings are non-negative circulations?</p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-exercise-circulations">
            <caption/>
            <sbsgroup widths="48% 48%">
              <sidebyside>
                <figure>
                  <caption/>
                  <image>
                    <shortdescription>Graph a: a four-vertex directed graph with edge weights 4, 4, 2, 2, and 6.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="exercise-circulation-a">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-1.75) rectangle (3.8,1.75);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (1.7,1.35) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (3.4,0) {$c$};
    \node[flow vertex] (d) at (1.7,-1.35) {$d$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$4$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$4$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below left] {$2$} (d);
    \draw[flow edge] (d) -- node[flow weight,below right] {$2$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- node[flow weight,above] {$6$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                  </image>
                </figure>
                <figure>
                  <caption/>
                  <image>
                    <shortdescription>Graph b: a four-vertex directed graph with edge weights 4, 4, 2, 2, and 5.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="exercise-circulation-b">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-1.75) rectangle (3.8,1.75);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (1.7,1.35) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (3.4,0) {$c$};
    \node[flow vertex] (d) at (1.7,-1.35) {$d$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$4$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$4$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below left] {$2$} (d);
    \draw[flow edge] (d) -- node[flow weight,below right] {$2$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (c) -- node[flow weight,above] {$5$} (a);
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                  </image>
                </figure>
              </sidebyside>
              <sidebyside>
                <figure>
                  <caption/>
                  <image>
                    <shortdescription>Graph c: a four-vertex directed acyclic graph in which every edge has weight 0.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="exercise-circulation-c">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-1.75) rectangle (3.8,1.75);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (1.7,1.35) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (3.4,0) {$c$};
    \node[flow vertex] (d) at (1.7,-1.35) {$d$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$0$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$0$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below left] {$0$} (d);
    \draw[flow edge] (d) -- node[flow weight,below right] {$0$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above] {$0$} (c);
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                  </image>
                </figure>
                <figure>
                  <caption/>
                  <image>
                    <shortdescription>Graph d: a directed triangle with edge weights 2, 2, and negative 2.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image label="exercise-circulation-d">
\begin{tikzpicture}
    \path[use as bounding box] (-0.4,-1.75) rectangle (3.8,1.75);
    \node[flow vertex] (a) at (0,-0.7) {$a$};
    \node[flow vertex] (b) at (1.7,1.1) {$b$};
    \node[flow vertex] (c) at (3.4,-0.7) {$c$};
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,above left] {$2$} (b);
    \draw[flow edge] (b) -- node[flow weight,above right] {$2$} (c);
    \draw[flow edge] (a) -- node[flow weight,below] {$-2$} (c);
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                  </image>
                </figure>
              </sidebyside>
            </sbsgroup>
          </figure>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        Let <m>W \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n}</m> be a linear subspace, and consider
        <m>W_{\geq 0} = W \cap \mathbb{R}^{n}_{\geq 0}</m>. An <term>elementary vector</term>
        <m>\vec{w} \in W_{\geq 0}\setminus\{\vec{0}\}</m> is a nonzero vector that is support
        minimal in the sense that there does not exist a nonzero vector
        <m>\vec{u} \in W_{\geq 0}</m> such that
        <m>\operatorname{supp}(\vec{u}) \subsetneq \operatorname{supp}(\vec{w})</m>.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-elementary-vectors">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>W \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n}</m> be a linear subspace and
            <m>W_{\geq 0} = W \cap \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{n}</m>.
          </p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Show that the support of any nonzero vector in <m>W_{\geq 0}</m> contains the
                support of some elementary vector.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Show that, for any nonzero vector <m>\vec{v} \in W_{\geq 0}</m>, there exists an
                elementary vector <m>\vec{u} \in W_{\geq 0}</m> such that
                <m>\vec{v} - \vec{u} \in W_{\geq 0}</m> and
                <m>\operatorname{supp}(\vec{v} - \vec{u}) \subsetneq
                \operatorname{supp}(\vec{v})</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Show that <m>W_{\geq 0}</m> is the set of non-negative linear combinations of its
                elementary vectors.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>
